 A peer asks for one work by hash and gets that work or nothing.

 The verb is the point. FactSync could already reconcile two stores, but a sync
 offer publishes your whole store to ask a question and the reply serves the
 answerer's whole store back. This asks for one hash.

 What is pinned here, in order of how badly it would hurt to lose:

 A LYING PEER IS CAUGHT BY ARITHMETIC, NOT BY TRUST. accept-reply hashes the work
 that came back and refuses it unless it addresses the hash that was asked for.
 Three ways to lie are tried: send a different work, forge content under the
 asked-for hash, and answer a question nobody asked. All three are refused, and
 none of the refusals needs to know anything about the peer. This is what lets
 the transport be untrusted.

 The fourth case is a correction, and it is worth keeping. Appending bytes to the
 reply is NOT corruption and is NOT refused: the work's content is
 length-prefixed on the wire, so a decoder takes exactly the bytes the length
 names and trailing garbage never reaches the work. A first cut of this test
 called that a corrupted work and expected a refusal; the code was right and the
 test was wrong. Corrupting a work means changing the content the hash addresses,
 which is what the forgery case does.

 A miss is empty, not an error. A peer that does not hold a work is ordinary.

 And the wire round-trips: the request and the reply survive encode/decode
 through the same framing every other agent message uses, because a verb that
 only works in-process is not a verb.

Section: Entry

  seed-bytes : List Integer = [3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3]

  filler : Integer, Text -> Text
  filler (n) (acc) = if n <= 0 then acc else filler (n - 1) (acc & "source line ")

  body : Text -> Text
  body (name) = name & ": " & filler 8 ""

  mk-def : Text, Text -> SourceDefinition
  mk-def (path) (content) =
    SourceDefinition {
      sd-hash = "", sd-path = path, sd-quire = "Test", sd-chapter = "C",
      sd-signer = "", sd-signature = [], sd-author = "", sd-timestamp = 0,
      sd-content = content
    }

  put : DiskFactStore, Keypair, Text, Text, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  put (st) (kp) (path) (content) (ts) =
    repo-persist-save-definition st (sign-definition kp (mk-def path content) ts) ts

 The wire, both ways: encode the message to a tag and a body, then decode it
 back. Anything that survives this is a real message and not a record that
 happens to be in scope.

  wire : AgentMessage -> AgentMessage
  wire (m) = decode-agent-msg (encode-agent-msg m) (encode-agent-msg-body m)

  wired-reply : AgentMessage -> WorkReplyMsg
  wired-reply (m) =
    when m
      is MsgWorkReply (r) -> r
      is otherwise -> WorkReplyMsg { from = AgentIdentity { public-key = [] }, hash = "WRONG TYPE", payload = "" }

  wired-request : AgentMessage -> WorkRequestMsg
  wired-request (m) =
    when m
      is MsgWorkRequest (r) -> r
      is otherwise -> WorkRequestMsg { from = AgentIdentity { public-key = [] }, hash = "WRONG TYPE" }

  got : Maybe SourceDefinition, Text -> Text
  got (m) (want) =
    when m
      is None -> "refused"
      is Just (d) -> if d.sd-content == want then "the work" else "WRONG WORK"

 The work is split across functions rather than one long chain of lets. A first
 cut put all of it in `opening` -- twenty-one bindings deep -- and the COMPILER
 crashed on it (`!EXC=54`, a wild read). The Developer's Guide warns that twenty
 or more chained lets nest the scope too deep; that is not a style note.

  server-id : AgentIdentity = AgentIdentity { public-key = [9] }
  client-id : AgentIdentity = AgentIdentity { public-key = [8] }

  seeded : Keypair -> DiskFactStore
  seeded (kp) =
    let s0 = disk-init
    in let s1 = put s0 kp "a.codex" (body "alpha") 10
    in put s1 kp "b.codex" (body "beta") 20

  served : WorkIndex, Text -> WorkReplyMsg
  served (ix) (h) = wired-reply (wire (MsgWorkReply (serve-work ix server-id (wired-request (wire (MsgWorkRequest (ask-work client-id h)))))))

  claiming : Text, Text -> WorkReplyMsg
  claiming (h) (payload) = WorkReplyMsg { from = server-id, hash = h, payload = payload }

  forged-payload : Keypair, Text -> Text
  forged-payload (kp) (h) =
    source-def-to-text (SourceDefinition {
      sd-hash = h, sd-path = "a.codex", sd-quire = "Test", sd-chapter = "C",
      sd-signer = kp.fingerprint, sd-signature = [], sd-author = kp.fingerprint,
      sd-timestamp = 10, sd-content = body "alpha" & " tampered"
    })

  report : WorkIndex, Keypair -> [Console.Write] Nothing
  report (ix) (kp) =
    let want = fact-content-hash (body "alpha")
    in let other = fact-content-hash (body "beta")
    in let reply = served ix want
    in let miss = served ix (fact-content-hash "never stored")
    in let swapped = served ix other
    in act
      print-line-uni ("request survives the wire: " & show ((wired-request (wire (MsgWorkRequest (ask-work client-id want)))).hash == want))
      print-line-uni ("type name: " & msg-type-name (MsgWorkRequest (ask-work client-id want)))
      print-line-uni ("served: " & got (accept-reply reply want) (body "alpha"))
      print-line-uni ("miss is empty, not an error: " & show (reply-is-empty miss))
      print-line-uni ("miss refused: " & got (accept-reply miss (fact-content-hash "never stored")) (body "alpha"))
      print-line-uni ("peer sends a DIFFERENT work: " & got (accept-reply (claiming want (swapped.payload)) want) (body "alpha"))
      print-line-uni ("peer FORGES content under the asked-for hash: " & got (accept-reply (claiming want (forged-payload kp want)) want) (body "alpha"))
      print-line-uni ("peer answers a question nobody asked: " & got (accept-reply (claiming other (reply.payload)) want) (body "alpha"))
      print-line-uni ("trailing bytes are ignored, not corruption: " & got (accept-reply (claiming want (reply.payload & "x")) want) (body "alpha"))
      print-line-uni ("the store serves what is named: " & got (accept-reply swapped other) (body "beta"))
    end

  opening : [Device.Block, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let kp = generate-keypair seed-bytes
    in let m = manifest-add manifest-empty (kp.fingerprint) (kp.public-key) 10000
    in let ix = repo-index-verified (seeded kp) m 5000
    in report ix kp
  end
